course: biology
lesson: 115
title: Why zoos exist and what critics say
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Zoos began as collections for kings to show off their power: for about 600 years English kings kept lions, an elephant and even a polar bear at the Tower of London, in small cages with poor care.
explain: Today good zoos say they are for four things: saving species, teaching people, doing research and helping people love animals, and the Arabian oryx, the condor and Przewalski's horse are alive in the wild partly thanks to them.
explain: But some people think it is wrong to keep wild animals at all, even well cared for, because they lose their freedom and many are not endangered and will never go home. Both sides have real points, and a clever thinker can see each.
explain: You can judge a zoo yourself: is it accredited, do the animals have space and things to do, and does it say no to selfies with tiger cubs and to visitors tapping on the glass?
fact: Modern zoos say they exist for conservation, education, research and helping people love animals; the oldest zoo still open is Tiergarten Schönbrunn in Vienna, opened in 1752.
fact: For about 600 years English kings kept lions, a polar bear and an elephant at the Tower of London; the polar bear was allowed to fish in the River Thames on a long chain.
fact: In the past many zoos kept animals in small, bare concrete cages, but science has shown that animals need space, choice and things to do.
fact: Some people think keeping wild animals in captivity is wrong even with good care, and some want zoos turned into sanctuaries that only rescue animals.
fact: Zoos have helped save the Arabian oryx, the California condor and Przewalski's horse, and for some species the zoo population is the only safe group left.
fact: Many zoos have stopped keeping elephants, orcas and polar bears if they cannot give them enough space, because these animals often suffered in small spaces (a wild orca swims huge distances in the open ocean); SeaWorld stopped breeding orcas in 2016.
fact: Good zoos are accredited by associations such as the AZA in America, BIAZA in Britain and Ireland, EAZA in Europe and the ZAA in Australia, which check care, safety and conservation; many roadside zoos are not.
fact: Paying to hold a tiger cub for a selfie often harms it: cubs are taken from their mothers early, bred for money and kept badly when they grow up, and good zoos never allow it.
fact: Studies suggest many visitors learn something about animals at zoos, though not all, and critics answer that people can learn from nature films too.
fact: Zoo research on health, behaviour and breeding can help wild animals; blood tests on zoo elephants helped scientists understand a deadly elephant virus.
fact: In the cartoon Madagascar the zoo animals escape and find the wild scary and hard, which echoes a real problem: zoo-born animals often cannot survive in the wild.
fact: Tapping on glass, shouting and flash photos frighten animals, which cannot leave as easily as visitors can.
exercise: Which four things do modern zoos say they are for? | Conservation, education, research and helping people love animals | Selling toys, food, tickets and parking | Training wild animals to perform in circuses | Keeping animals for kings and queens
exercise: What is the oldest zoo still open today? | Tiergarten Schönbrunn in Vienna, opened in 1752 | London Zoo, opened in 1066 | San Diego Zoo, opened in 1916 | The Tower of London zoo, still open now beside the river
exercise: What did English kings keep at the Tower of London for about 600 years? | Lions, a polar bear and an elephant | Only hunting dogs and horses for the king | Dinosaur bones dug up from the river | Penguins and seals in a big stone pool
exercise: Why did zoos change from small concrete cages? | Science showed animals need space, choice and things to do | Concrete became too expensive | Animals asked for bigger homes | Visitors wanted to see less of the animals and more of the gardens
exercise: What do some animal rights groups want zoos to become? | Sanctuaries that only rescue animals | Bigger zoos with many more small cages | Shops selling exotic pets to families | Circuses that travel all over the world
exercise: Which animals have zoos helped save from extinction? | Arabian oryx, California condor and Przewalski's horse | Dodo, mammoth and sabre-toothed cat | House mouse, pigeon and rat | Lions and tigers, which have never been in any danger at all
exercise: Why have many zoos stopped keeping elephants and orcas? | They need huge space and often suffered in small enclosures | They are far too boring for visitors to bother coming in to see | They eat less than other animals | They are not real animals
exercise: What did SeaWorld stop in 2016? | Breeding orcas | Selling tickets | Feeding dolphins | Keeping any fish
exercise: What does it mean if a zoo is accredited? | An association checked it meets rules for care, safety and conservation | It has the most animals in the country | It is owned by the king | It lets visitors feed and stroke the animals
exercise: Why is a «selfie with a tiger cub» not harmless? | Cubs are taken from mothers early and bred for money | Tigers like posing for photos and are happy to be held | It helps tigers in the wild | The cubs are always toys
exercise: What do critics say about learning at zoos? | People can learn from nature films without keeping animals | Nobody ever learns anything at a zoo | Books are banned at zoos | Animals teach children much better than any person or book ever can
exercise: How did zoo research help wild elephants? | Blood tests helped scientists understand a deadly elephant virus | Zoo elephants were trained to find poachers | Zoo elephants were sent to teach wild ones | Researchers gave all the wild elephants in Africa tablets every week
exercise: What problem does the cartoon Madagascar show that is real? | Zoo-born animals often cannot cope with the wild | Lions can live happily on fruit from the jungle trees | All zoo animals secretly want to go to New York City | Zebras can talk to penguins in their own language
exercise: Why should visitors not tap on the glass? | It frightens animals, which cannot leave as easily as people | It wakes the sleeping animals up in good time for their feeding | The glass might turn cloudy | It makes the animals hungry
exercise: Why could both sides in the zoo argument be partly right? | Zoos save some species, but many zoo animals lose freedom and never go home | One side is always completely wrong | They are talking about different zoos | Nobody can know anything about zoos
test: «zoos are just prisons for animals, end of» How could a fair thinker answer? | Some zoos were cruel, but good zoos help save species; people still disagree | Yes, every zoo in history is exactly the same | No, every animal loves being in a zoo | Zoos are really just big parks
test: «my uncle says zoos were always about saving animals» What is right? | Zoos began as kings' collections to show off power | Yes, from the very first day | Zoos began as hospitals for sick pets and farm animals | Zoos began as farms for food
test: Alex the lion in Madagascar can't hunt in the wild. What would happen to a real zoo-born lion set free? | It might struggle to find food and survive | It would hunt perfectly well from the very first day | It would catch a bus back to the zoo by itself | It would live happily on wild fruit for years
test: How could you tell a good zoo from a bad one on a visit? | Check it is accredited and its animals have space and things to do | Count how many animals do tricks | See if it sells the cheapest ice cream | Check if you are allowed to touch and feed everything you see there
test: At a holiday resort a stall offers «hold a baby tiger for €10». What is the best thing to do? | Say no, because such places breed and use cubs for money | Hold it gently and pay double, so the cub gets lots more food | Buy the cub to save it | Feed it chocolate first
test: «bloke kept banging the glass at the gorillas, keeper went mad, bit much innit» Was the keeper right? | Yes, banging frightens animals that cannot get away | No, gorillas enjoy the noise | No, glass blocks all sound | Yes, but only because the glass is expensive
test: Which animal would be the hardest to keep well in a zoo? | An orca, which swims huge distances in the ocean | A tortoise | A stick insect | A meerkat
test: The Tower of London polar bear fished in the Thames on a chain. What would people think today? | That it was cruel and far from what a polar bear needs | That it was the perfect home | That polar bears like muddy city rivers better than the sea | That it should be done again
test: Why might some things be easier to learn about in a zoo than in the jungle? | Animals can be watched and checked up close, day after day | Zoo animals tell the scientists all their secrets in the end | Jungles are closed to all scientists | Animals in the jungle do not exist
test: «4 elephants in the old zoo picture, how many trunks and how many tails altogether» | 8, four trunks and four tails | 4, the trunk is counted as a tail | 16, two trunks and two tails each | 12, two trunks and one tail each
test: «Madagascar zoo had a lion, a zebra, a hippo and a giraffe, how many legs is that, no, add the 4 penguins» | 24, sixteen for the four mammals and eight for the penguins | 16, four legs for each of the mammals | 32, four legs for every animal | 20, four for each mammal and one for each penguin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the historical origins of zoological collections? | They originated as collections kept by rulers as a demonstration of power | They originated as institutions for the reintroduction of species | They originated as veterinary hospitals for domestic animals | They originated as agricultural establishments for the production of meat and milk
exam: Identify the principal objection advanced by critics of zoological institutions. | Wild animals are deprived of their liberty, and many are neither endangered nor ever released | Zoological institutions charge excessive fees for admission | Zoological institutions are insufficiently numerous | Animals in zoological institutions are excessively free
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning accredited zoological institutions. | They are inspected by associations for standards of care, safety and conservation | They are exempt from all inspection | They are permitted to offer photographs with tiger cubs and with other young wild animals | They are owned by the royal household
exam: Determine the reason for which certain institutions have ceased to maintain orcas and elephants. | Such species require extensive space and have frequently suffered in confinement | Such species are no longer of any interest whatsoever to the visiting public or to the press | Such species are now extinct in the wild | Such species are legally classified as domestic animals
